response:
 Here, we solve the system of congruences step-by-step. First, we simplify each congruence. The first congruence is \(7x \equiv 21 \pmod{14}\). Dividing both sides by 7, we get \(x \equiv 3 \pmod{2}\). The second congruence is \(2x + 13 \equiv 16 \pmod{9}\). Subtracting 13 from both sides, we get \(2x \equiv 3 \pmod{9}\). To find the inverse of 2 modulo 9, we note that \(2 \cdot 5 \equiv 1 \pmod{9}\). So, multiplying both sides by 5, we get \(x \equiv 5 \pmod{9}\). The third congruence is \(-2x + 1 \equiv x \pmod{25}\). Adding \(2x\) to both sides, we get \(1 \equiv 3x \pmod{25}\). To find the inverse of 3 modulo 25, we note that \(3 \cdot 17 \equiv 1 \pmod{25}\). So, multiplying both sides by 17, we get \(x \equiv 17 \pmod{25}\). Now we have the simplified system: \[
\begin{align*}
x &\equiv 3 \pmod{2} \\
x &\equiv 5 \pmod{9} \\
x &\equiv 17 \pmod{25} \\
\end{align*}
\] Using the Chinese Remainder Theorem, we need to find a common solution. We start by finding a solution to the first two congruences: \[
\begin{align*}
x &\equiv 3 \pmod{2} \\
x &\equiv 5 \pmod{9} \\
\end{align*}
\] The solution to this system is of the form \(x = 9k + 5\). We need \(9k + 5 \equiv 3 \pmod{2}\), which simplifies to \(k \equiv 0 \pmod{2}\) or \(k = 2m\). So, \(x = 18m + 5\). Now we check the third congruence: \[
18m + 5 \equiv 17 \pmod{25}
\] Simplifying, we get \(18m \equiv 12 \pmod{25}\). Dividing both sides by 3, we get \(6m \equiv 4 \pmod{25}\). To find the inverse of 6 modulo 25, we note that \(6 \cdot 14 \equiv 1 \pmod{25}\). So, multiplying both sides by 14, we get \(m \equiv 14 \cdot 4 \equiv 56 \equiv 6 \pmod{25}\). So, \(m = 25n + 6\), and substituting this back, we get \(x = 18(25n + 6) + 5 = 450n + 113\). The smallest positive four-digit number of this form is obtained by setting \(n = 2\), giving \(x = 450 \cdot 2 + 113 = 1013\). \end{think} <answer> 1013 </answer>

